        ["Why is the test error so much higher than in cross-validation?", "The test years were strong (+21.6% against +0.9% in training). A forecast that knew each test year's average would score 30.9 instead of 37.2. On top, the models cannot rank firms (rank correlation about -0.02)."],
        ["Why is R2 negative?", "R2 compares with the test years' own average. All forecasts are near the training average, far below the test years' +21.6%, so everything, even the naive forecast, looks bad on that scale."],
        ["So are the models useless?", "On this data they show no skill out of sample. That is common for 12-month stock returns, and the assignment accepts it as a result."],
