    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ PART 3
    ("h1", "Part 3. The script, word for word (about 8 minutes)"),
    ("small", "Speak at a calm pace (about 130 words a minute). Words in italics in square brackets are stage directions, not spoken. Target times are cumulative and include short pauses; they replace the rough times in the slide notes."),
    ("h2", "Slide 1: title (0:40, until 0:40)"),
    ("p", "Good morning. We are Group 8. Our question is simple: can an investor use ESG scores, or forecasts built on them, to earn extra return, and can the data be trusted for that purpose? Our short answer is on the slide: the data had seven problems, no model beat a naive forecast, the Environmental score went with higher returns only in Europe from 2021, and the backtest edge cannot be told apart from noise. We would not invest on this evidence. Here is why."),
    ("h2", "Slide 2: four questions and the data (0:50, until 1:30)"),
    ("p", "We asked four questions. First, can the data be trusted? Second, can a model forecast twelve-month returns better than simply using the average? Third, where, if anywhere, was the Environmental score associated with higher returns? And fourth, does a trading rule with costs and an ESG floor beat a simple equal-weight benchmark? *[point to the cards]* The data comes from the course: 1,376 firms over fiscal years 2012 to 2025, five files, about 172,000 monthly returns. It is simulated, so the exercise tests our method, not the real market. The dataset and its licence are cited at the bottom of every slide."),
    ("h2", "Slide 3: seven data problems (1:25, until 2:55)"),
    ("p", "Before any model, we checked all five files and found seven problems. *[point to the highlighted row]* The most dangerous one is this column, Analyst_Outlook. It matches the next year's return with a rank correlation of 0.68, and earlier years with about zero. It behaves as if someone wrote it with next year's results in hand. A model that uses it cuts the forecast error from 37.6 to 29.1, but no investor had it on 1 July, so we removed it. The other problems: 28 firms report sales and market value about a thousand times too high; we saw it because their carbon, water and energy figures were normal for their real size, and we corrected it; 300 monthly returns appeared twice; the list of current firms contains only survivors, so we used all firms; and the restated ESG scores behave as if they contain hindsight, so we used the original ones. *[point to the pipeline]* Our pipeline follows one rule: the yearly report is out by the end of May, we decide on 1 July and hold for twelve months. We learn from 2015 to 2021 and test on 2022 to 2024, and the test years are used only for reporting."),
    ("h2", "Slide 4: models (1:05, until 4:00)"),
    ("p", "We compared linear regression with two tree models, a random forest and gradient boosting, each next to a naive forecast that predicts the training average for every firm. Cross-validation picks linear regression, but the three models are within 0.05 points: a tie. *[point to the yellow bar]* On the test years, no model beats the naive forecast: 37.2 for the naive forecast, the yellow bar, against 37.8 to 38.1 for the models. An error this large means these forecasts cannot be used to size or rank positions. Why is the error so much higher than in cross-validation? Mainly because the test years were strong: an average twelve-month return of 21.6 percent, against 0.9 percent in training. And the models cannot rank firms either; the rank correlation is about minus 0.02 and changes sign from year to year. So the extra complexity of the tree models does not pay off."),
    ("h2", "Slide 5: Environmental score (1:20, until 5:20)"),
    ("p", "Next we asked where a better Environmental score went with higher returns. For each region and period we compared every firm with peers in the same industry and the same year. Pooled over all regions, nothing is distinguishable from zero. *[point to the yellow bar]* Region by region, one cell stands out: Europe from 2021. A firm about 17 score points better than average earned about 3.1 percentage points more over the next twelve months than its peers. *[point to Latin America]* The second-longest bar, Latin America, is not distinguishable from zero; its interval includes zero. How sure are we about Europe? After correcting for the fourteen cells, p is 0.018. But if we treat each of the four years as one piece of evidence, p is about 0.09, and we picked this cell after looking at all fourteen. So: plausible, not proven, and an association, not a return shown to be tradable. With the restated scores the estimate jumps to 4.88: that is what hindsight does to a result."),
    ("h2", "Slide 6: backtest (1:20, until 6:40)"),
    ("p", "Finally, a trading rule. Each 1 July we ranked firms by the forecast, trained only on earlier years, bought the top ten percent, held them for twelve months and paid trading costs of 20 basis points per unit of turnover, from 2018 to 2024. The ESG floor first removes firms with a low Environmental score. *[point to the table]* The rules earned 9.4, 9.6 and 11.4 percent a year, against 8.8 percent for the benchmark. That looks good, but the best rule's advantage in risk-adjusted terms, a Sharpe difference of plus 0.11, has a 95 percent range from minus 0.16 to plus 0.38. It includes zero, so we cannot tell it from luck, and 11 to 19 percent of random portfolios of the same size do at least as well. *[point to the floor card]* The floor did not cost return, but it cost concentration, volatility and trading. And it is not where the gain comes from: holding all high-score firms earns about the benchmark, and without formation year 2022 every rule is below the benchmark."),
    ("h2", "Slide 7: what would change with real data (1:10, until 7:50)"),
    ("p", "What would change with real data? Four things are hard even after an audit like ours. Real ESG raters disagree; six major raters agree only 0.38 to 0.71. Vendors rewrite old scores, and we saw how much that flatters a result. Survivorship is harder to see in real files, and real trading costs are not a constant. *[point to the table]* Which conclusions survive? That no model beats the naive forecast likely survives. Our checks survive, the specific errors do not. Survivor and hindsight bias survive in direction, not in size. The Europe result does not survive as a signal: the course's starter notebook told us one real relationship was planted; with real data nobody tells you that. And the floor may not stay free. So our recommendation: we would not invest on this evidence before we have several raters, dated score versions, delisting returns, realistic costs and a replication of the Europe result."),
    ("h2", "Slide 8: conclusion (0:10, until 8:00)"),
    ("p", "To sum up: we would not allocate on this evidence. Thank you; we are happy to take your questions."),

